{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-132112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)$ at the point $r(\\pi )$ if the curve and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given both the curve $r(t)$ and the derivative ${T}^{′}(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $r(\\pi )$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-132112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,-\\frac{4}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\frac{8}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}),\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2s}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8}{25})}=\\frac{25}{8}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-64738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{25}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{25}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡s),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡s+{cos}^{2}⁡s+2{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡s+2{sin}^{2}⁡s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2({cos}^{2}⁡s+{sin}^{2}⁡s)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-132105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\sqrt{3})⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s}}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s}}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{s})}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{s})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\sqrt{s}+{cos}^{2}⁡\\sqrt{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{s}})}=4\\sqrt{s}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-132110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2s"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{s}"] ["math" "4{s}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{s}}"] ["math" "\\frac{s}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\,k\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature: \n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{κ(s)}=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})k \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j] \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s)=\\frac{1}{‖{T}^{′}(s)‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{9})}=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-64732" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64732" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64732" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(3,1)$ where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is $\\,T(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,1⟩,$ since the point $P(3,1)$ is at the tip of $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨-1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=⟨-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+R(\\frac{\\pi }{2})N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨3,1⟩+2⟨-1,0⟩=⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64836" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-64836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $1$"] ["text" "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{10}$"] ["text" "The center is $(1,0)$ and the radius is $1$"] ["text" "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $2$"] ["text" "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-64836" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The center is $(1,1)$ and the radius is $2$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ where $t=\\pi $, given that $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖=2,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(\\pi )=⟨\\pi ,2⟩$,  since the point $P(\\pi ,2)$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=\\frac{1}{2}⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=\\pi $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{T}^{′}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}⟨0,-1⟩=⟨0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi )+R(\\pi )N(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2⟩+4⟨0,-1⟩=⟨\\pi ,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97594" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-97594" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97594" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The center is $(\\pi ,-1)$ and the radius $2$"] ["text" "The center is $(\\pi ,-2)$ and the radius $4$"] ["text" "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $4$"] ["text" "The center is $(\\pi ,0)$ and the radius $\\frac{1}{4}$"] ["text" "The center is $(\\pi ,1)$ and the radius $2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97594" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The center is $(\\pi ,-2)$ and the radius $4$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(0,0)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=1,$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩,$ since the point $P(0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0,0⟩+1\\cdot ⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-132204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The center is $⟨0,-1⟩$ and the radius is $1$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨1,-1⟩$ and the radius is $2$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $1$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $2$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨1,1⟩$ and the radius is $1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132204" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The center is $⟨1,0⟩$ and the radius is $1$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0)$ where $t=0$, given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t),$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,0⟩,$ since the point $P(1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t),$ ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,0⟩+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟨-1,1⟩=⟨0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-97593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The center is $(0,-1)$ and the radius $2$"] ["text" "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius $2$"] ["text" "The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$"] ["text" "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-97593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The center is $(0,1)$ and the radius $\\sqrt{2}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $r(t)$ at the point $P(1,0)$ where $t=0,$ given that $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖=\\sqrt{2},$ and the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ to the curve is\n\n$$\nT(t)=⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}⟩.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given both the value of $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$ and the unit tangent $T(t)$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNotice that $r(0)=⟨1,0⟩,$ since the point $P(1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we find ${T}^{′}(t)$, ${T}^{′}(0)$ and $‖{T}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+1}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{{({e}^{2t}+1)}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\ {T}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{({e}^{0}+1)}^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{({e}^{0}+1)}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t=0$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨1,0⟩+2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩+2⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩+⟨2,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-132210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The center is $⟨-1,2⟩$ and the radius is $2\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨-1,4⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨-2,4⟩$ and the radius is $2\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨1,-2⟩$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "The center is $⟨3,-2⟩$ and the radius is $2\\sqrt{2}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The center is $⟨3,-2⟩$ and the radius is $2\\sqrt{2}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨4{t}^{2},\\,4t⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =4⟨2(0),\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =4⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =8⟨1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =4⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\times 8⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =32\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =32\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-32k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{3}}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{32} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-132128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-98668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-98668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t),$ we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ),\\,{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\pi },\\,-{e}^{\\pi }⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨-1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & ={e}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-2{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,2{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{\\pi }⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times {e}^{\\pi }⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & ={({e}^{\\pi })}^{2}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ \\,0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ \\,0 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\pi }\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2{e}^{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi })}^{3}}{\\,2{e}^{2\\pi }\\,}=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-98668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-98668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-98668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{2\\pi }"] ["math" "2{e}^{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }"] ["math" "{e}^{2\\pi }"] ["math" "{e}^{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-98668" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi }"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-132130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-132130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\pi $ for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{cos}^{2}⁡t,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi ,\\,-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0+1,{e}^{-1}+0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{e},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1/e\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{e}\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{e})}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-35234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "e"] ["math" "{e}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and ${r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "436f8bbc4262ae6cdb18d781493272bb9a42d35ccf65773daf901a3e93fe7f01"} {"question" "q-35114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "q-35114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/state.json" "file_sha256" "e6addba3b654af48ade50b6d91bdb852e8b7d865c92f9f563c2a6e5c4b7a9182"} {"question" "e-3338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s.$ Find the radius of curvature of $\\mathbf r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that  its  unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/example-3338.json" "file_sha256" "003c8abe0d13242be366d20e7734b4ed67ffd85e037cb4d59ae55fb33569f6e9"} {"question" "e-3338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s,$ we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,i-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})\\,j \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(s)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{s}{3}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/example-3338.json" "file_sha256" "003c8abe0d13242be366d20e7734b4ed67ffd85e037cb4d59ae55fb33569f6e9"} {"question" "e-3339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at the point $\\mathbf{r}(0),$ if the curve and the derivative $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t, \\:\\dfrac{ t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf T'(t)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^3}}\\left\\langle -t, \\:  1\\right\\rangle \\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/example-3339.json" "file_sha256" "e222d0cf4a3b7d87d5e92b2e7884a1bf0a00d072aa3932664c9f9487d091e887"} {"question" "e-3339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given both the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ and the derivative $\\mathbf T'(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula to compute the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mathbf \\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨1,t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate $\\mathbf T'(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1+{0}^{2})^{3}}}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(0)}{‖{T}^{′}(0)‖}=⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & = \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩+1\\cdot ⟨0,1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/assets/cc9e1eaf1dc8655565a94b7ef0146dc2bb458cc7376828b19352acc01ddbeaad.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/example-3339.json" "file_sha256" "e222d0cf4a3b7d87d5e92b2e7884a1bf0a00d072aa3932664c9f9487d091e887"} {"question" "e-3340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle e^{ t}, \\: e^{- t} \\right\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/example-3340.json" "file_sha256" "db72d0f64bfc82fdaab48a9e33bb36b3c692d6f84dd6762c37eb764b39c2548a"} {"question" "e-3340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we are given only the curve $\\mathbf r(t),$ we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(0),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-(-{e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(0)$ and $\\mathbf r''(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\,-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\,-1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}=\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{3}}{\\,2\\,}=\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069132/example-3340.json" "file_sha256" "db72d0f64bfc82fdaab48a9e33bb36b3c692d6f84dd6762c37eb764b39c2548a"}] "usage" {"q-35234" [] "e-3339" [] "e-3340" [] "e-3338" []} "basis" 2348 "retractions" [[17592186120311 :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t = π$ for the curve $r(t) = ⟨e^{\\sin t}, e^{\\cos t}⟩$."] [17592186119501 :question/problem "Consider a curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$. Find the radius of curvature of $\\mathbf r(s)$ at an arbitrary point knowing that its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ is given by\n$T(s)|=-\\sin (\\frac{s}{3})i + \\cos (\\frac{s}{3})j$."] [17592186119501 :question/worked-solution "Since the curve $\\mathbf{r}(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(s)$ are both parametrized by arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate $\\mathbf T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and calculate its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(s) &= \\frac{d}{ds}(-\\sin (\\frac{s}{3}))i + \\frac{d}{ds}(\\cos (\\frac{s}{3}))j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\cos (\\frac{s}{3})i - \\frac{1}{3}\\sin (\\frac{s}{3})j \\\\\n‖T^{′}(s)‖ &= \\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cos (\\frac{s}{3}))^{2} + (-\\frac{1}{3}\\sin (\\frac{s}{3}))^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}(\\cos^{2} (\\frac{s}{3}) + \\sin^{2} (\\frac{s}{3}))} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac{1}{\\|\\mathbf T'(s)\\|} = \\dfrac{1}{\\:\\dfrac{1}{3}\\:} = 3\\,\n$$"] [17592186119502 :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the osculating circle to the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at the point $\\mathbf{r}(0)$, if the curve and the derivative $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ of the corresponding unit tangent vector are given by\n\n$\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t, \\:\\dfrac{t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf T'(t)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{(t^2+1)^3}}\\left\\langle -t, \\: 1\\right\\rangle \\,$."] [17592186119502 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ and the derivative $\\mathbf T'(t)$ of the unit tangent, we will use the following formula to compute the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\mathbf \\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|}{\\left\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, $\\mathbf r'(0)$, and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨1, t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(0) &= ⟨1, 0⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 0^{2}} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate $\\mathbf T'(0)$ and find its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT^{′}(0) &= ⟨ - (0)/(\\sqrt{(1 + 0^{2})^{3}}), (1)/(\\sqrt{(1 + 0^{2})^{3}})⟩ = ⟨0, 1⟩ \\\\\n‖T^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + 1^{2}} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(0) &= (‖r^{′}(0)‖)/(‖T^{′}(0)‖) = \\frac{1}{1} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal normal vector at $t = 0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(0) &= (T^{′}(0))/(‖T^{′}(0)‖) = ⟨0, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(0) + R(0)N(0) &=  \\\\\n⟨0, 0⟩ + 1 \\cdot ⟨0, 1⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1⟩ \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1834/Source/Images/7448afe0b58173006462d2b229836749.png)"] [17592186119503 :question/problem "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ for the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle e^{t}, \\: e^{-t} \\right\\rangle$."] [17592186119503 :question/worked-solution "Since we are given only the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$, $\\mathbf r'(0)$, and $\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}), \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{-t})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{t},-e^{-t}⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(0) &= ⟨e^{0},-e^{-0}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(0)‖ &= \\sqrt{(1)^{2} + (-1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}), \\frac{d}{dt}(-e^{-t})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{t}, - (-e^{-t})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨e^{t}, e^{-t}⟩ \\\\\nr^{″}(0) &= ⟨e^{0}, e^{-0}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(0)$ and $\\mathbf r''(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(0) \\cdot r^{″}(0) &= ⟨1,-1, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨1, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}0 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ = 1\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= 1 \\mid 1k \\\\\n&= 2k \\\\\n‖r^{′}(0) \\cdot r^{″}(0)‖ &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(0) &= (‖r^{′}(0)‖^{3})/(‖r^{′}(0) \\cdot r^{″}(0)‖) = ((\\sqrt{2})^{3})/(2) = \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186118342 17592186118343 17592186118344 17592186118345 17592186118346] "immutable_fields" [[17592186118341 :answer-field/correct] [17592186118341 :answer-field/id] [17592186118341 :answer-field/key] [17592186118341 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
